{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"685a657ce9ae2306dc40571fe56eda2c078fe242c38a38c8e4ab86caac438b7e","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_8497fc5e1f8906560f47a1045a81a0825034e8d56bea37823ce7cde5c37022ee","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a significant original contribution by formalizing nonparametric neural networks and providing a theoretical guarantee for finite optimal size. The introduction of AdaRad and CapNorm represents a novel methodological approach to handling dynamic architecture changes during training. The detailed analysis of training dynamics and the honest reporting of negative results on specific datasets further strengthen the originality and rigor of the work.","paper_id":"BJK3Xasel","paper_identity_sha256":"94be6539a8a036eb76274494349bc84b9c7f64b1a7a7a2e5c25799b6636070c1","prompt_identity_sha256":"5ffed268b9afa8518854c34e4125f56310a4b06305eac5575024e21c5f8df4ad","raw_response_sha256":"7293ae44ac649f7fe9afad0160f4af9f1b2181774641b7b4c3fa4d5365341c17","request_identity_sha256":"b782dd4c7837f30d3d228eb3755009d4fa2aca81540c07c89427cefde848fa6d","reviewer_identity_sha256":"6832030d37bc6b05f4de12f913ecdea182594ad5d7ac3dcd33edcb54d7bd3a1a","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v2","structural_context_sha256":"ec8cc86da9d81ce8a38b5be410cca5062f602d489d08646f9fd3ed8657b0909d","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-001","SRC-004","SRC-006","SRC-008"],"review_text":"The paper introduces a novel problem formulation by defining 'nonparametric neural networks,' where the dimensionality of the weight matrices is treated as an undetermined variable in the optimization process (SRC-006). This shifts the model selection task from a discrete, black-box search over hyperparameters to a continuous optimization problem over both weights and network size (SRC-004). The authors provide a theoretical foundation for this formulation by proving that a global minimum exists for finite dimensionality under specific fan-in or fan-out regularizers (SRC-008). This formalization addresses the high computational cost and inefficiency of traditional grid or random search methods by allowing the network size to adapt dynamically during a single training cycle (SRC-001).","subdimension_id":"ORIG-PROB"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-009","SRC-012","SRC-014","SRC-016"],"review_text":"The methodology introduces two distinct algorithmic components: CapNorm and AdaRad. CapNorm is a modified batch normalization layer that prevents the negation of the regularizer by capping the scaling of standard deviation, which is a necessary adaptation for nonparametric settings (SRC-012). More significantly, the paper proposes AdaRad (Adaptive Radial-Angular Gradient Descent), a novel optimization algorithm that decomposes the gradient into radial and angular components (SRC-014). This decomposition allows the algorithm to normalize the angular component for fast learning while preserving the radial component to maintain the shrinkage mechanism required for unit removal (SRC-014). The algorithm also incorporates a mechanism for adding and removing zero units to maintain f-equivalence, enabling dynamic architecture changes during training (SRC-009, SRC-016).","subdimension_id":"ORIG-MTH"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-024","SRC-025","SRC-027","SRC-029"],"review_text":"The paper provides a detailed analysis of the nonparametric training process, offering insights into the dynamics of unit addition and removal. It observes that nonparametric networks do not reach near-zero training error, unlike parametric networks, yet this does not harm validation performance (SRC-024). The analysis reveals that the validation error of nonparametric networks tends to improve slowly over many epochs, whereas unregularized parametric networks may exhibit increasing validation error in the long run (SRC-025). Furthermore, the authors analyze the 'life lengths' of neurons and the evolution of fan-in/fan-out lengths, noting that units added earlier are more likely to survive and that the network compensates for lower activation values in deeper layers by adjusting fan-in lengths (SRC-027, SRC-029). These observations deepen the understanding of how dynamic architecture changes interact with gradient-based optimization.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-ANL"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-019","SRC-021","SRC-023","SRC-026"],"review_text":"The experimental setup evaluates the proposed method on three standard benchmark datasets: MNIST, rectangles images, and convex (SRC-019). The evaluation protocol is rigorous, comparing nonparametric networks against parametric networks of the same size and against the best performers from an exhaustive random search (SRC-021). The paper includes a detailed case study analyzing a single training run on MNIST, providing visualizations of validation error, training error, layer sizes, and neuron life lengths (SRC-023, SRC-026). This level of granular analysis of the training dynamics is a valuable addition to the experimental evidence, going beyond simple performance metrics to illustrate the behavior of the nonparametric framework.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-011","SRC-012","SRC-014","SRC-031"],"review_text":"The paper creatively combines the concept of dynamic network growth (inspired by Wei et al., 2016) with a novel regularization and optimization strategy (SRC-031). The rationale for this combination is well-articulated: standard batch normalization would negate the regularizer, necessitating CapNorm, and standard gradient descent would not handle the addition of new units effectively, necessitating the radial-angular decomposition in AdaRad (SRC-012, SRC-014). The combination of f-equivalence for adding/removing units with the specific shrinkage operators for p=2 norm regularization creates a coherent system that allows for dense weight matrices while maintaining the ability to prune units (SRC-011). This synergy addresses the specific challenges of training networks with variable dimensions.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-COM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-022","SRC-024"],"review_text":"The paper provides a critical assessment of its own method's limitations by reporting negative results on the convex dataset, where nonparametric networks performed substantially worse than parametric networks of the same size (SRC-022). The authors acknowledge that the method's tendency to return small networks can hurt performance on tasks that require larger architectures, even if the architecture were re-trained in parametric mode (SRC-022). This honest reporting of failure cases, along with the observation that parametric training reaches good accuracy much faster than nonparametric training (SRC-024), adds credibility to the evaluation and highlights the current boundaries of the proposed framework.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-NEG"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"a1e0e72c42e36a9a1211dc43a9458996ff3ba0558e99ac1c9234a8702fdd3954","paper_id":"BJK3Xasel","paper_ordinal":27,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"7293ae44ac649f7fe9afad0160f4af9f1b2181774641b7b4c3fa4d5365341c17","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"BJK3Xasel","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"46c7061bf0714fe3a6c8549e9707505cfe04e05ac48f4b5f941ee1830e14a656","request_identity_sha256":"b782dd4c7837f30d3d228eb3755009d4fa2aca81540c07c89427cefde848fa6d","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v2","usage":{"completion_tokens":1481,"prompt_tokens":17359,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":18840},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"b782dd4c7837f30d3d228eb3755009d4fa2aca81540c07c89427cefde848fa6d","role":"ORIG","role_ordinal":3,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v1"}